  <h2>Sound natural: treat the chunk as one thought group</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Default prominence usually falls on the final important content word, but new contrast can move it. Keep articles, pronouns, and prepositions light enough for the phrase to cohere. Do not rush so much that the listener loses a fixed word.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Spoken line</th><th>Connection</th><th>Meaning focus</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>We're on the same <b>PAGE</b> about the <b>DATE</b>, / but not the <b>BUD</b>get.</td><td><span class="ex">on the</span> forms a light bridge into <span class="ex">same page</span></td><td>scope of agreement</td></tr>
    <tr><td>We won't cut <b>COR</b>ners on <b>AC</b>cess.</td><td><span class="ex">corners on</span> links smoothly across the word boundary</td><td>quality boundary</td></tr>
    <tr><td>That name rings a <b>BELL</b>.</td><td>the article <span class="ex">a</span> normally reduces to /ə/</td><td>partial recognition</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Let's get the ball <b>ROLL</b>ing.</td><td><span class="ex">Let's</span> is one contracted syllable</td><td>starting action</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Let's call it a <b>DAY</b>.</td><td><span class="ex">call it a</span> links as one lead-in</td><td>temporary stopping</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
