  <h2>Paraphrase before you reveal the expression</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-transform><p class="note-line">Respond aloud in plain language first. Then give one idiom, state its grammar and register, and return to literal detail. Reveal and compare.</p><div class="transform-list">
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>1</b> Confirm shared understanding without implying agreement about every project issue.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Let's make sure we're on the same page about the October 19 launch date.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>2</b> Praise the supplier and state the evidence that earns the idiom.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The supplier went the extra mile by delivering four days early without adding a labor charge.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>3</b> Reject an exaggerated total-restart claim after one connector fails.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>We are not back to square one: one connector needs rebuilding, while four other systems still work.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>4</b> Report encouraging staging results without guaranteeing live performance.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>So far, so good: the retest produced zero duplicates in 300 staging orders, but live reliability remains untested.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>5</b> Replace an informal idiom with wording suitable for the B2 evidence-based writing assessment.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The pilot provides limited evidence supporting a larger trial; it does not establish full effectiveness.</strong></p></div>
  </div></div>

  <h2>Practice figurative language without forcing personal disclosure</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>You never need to discuss a real conflict, workplace failure, medical result, legal problem, family disagreement, immigration history, financial loss, discrimination experience, confidential project, or belief associated with a culture-specific expression. Use Alder Street or a tutor-created situation. The skill is contextual inference and pragmatic choice, not revealing private experience or proving cultural belonging.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Context detective:</b> Hear an unfamiliar expression inside a four-sentence fictional story. Infer literal fit, outcome, attitude, and plain paraphrase before checking a reference.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Grammar switch:</b> Change tense, subject, participant, and complement around <span class="ex">on the same page, draw the line at, keep someone in the loop</span>, and <span class="ex">ring a bell</span> while preserving the fixed core.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Channel switch:</b> Express one project event in an internal chat, client update, public notice, and formal incident record. Remove every idiom that makes the critical fact harder to recover.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Restraint edit:</b> Cut four idioms from an overloaded paragraph. Keep the one that performs a necessary interactional job and paraphrase the rest.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Variation check:</b> Compare grain and pinch of salt or another documented variety difference. Name the target variety without declaring the alternative defective.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Final production: tell a two-minute idiom-controlled project story</h3><p>Use Alder Street or a new fictional project. Establish the people, relationship, channel, dates, goal, first action, setback, scope of lost progress, quality boundary, extra effort, current result, remaining uncertainty, and next action in clear literal language. Add no more than four expressions, each with a different job: alignment or initiation, difficulty or setback, boundary or effort, and provisional progress or temporary stopping.</p><p>For each expression, preserve its article, preposition, complement, pronoun slot, and verb form. Make the meaning inferable before explaining it. After the story, paraphrase every expression plainly, label the register, identify any overstatement or implied agency, and replace one phrase for a client update or formal record. Use natural thought groups, one function-word reduction, and one contrastive prominence change. Your listener changes the channel, stakes, evidence, or relationship; revise immediately.</p><p><b>Listener check:</b> Can the listener recover each expression's meaning, communicative job, grammar, degree, and register from the surrounding facts, and does the plain version preserve the same claim without losing essential dates, standards, agency, or uncertainty?</p></div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You preserved contextual inference, fixed grammar, acceptable variation, register, respectful pragmatics, evidence scope, and the B2 assessment's plain evidence-based style."><h3>Seven final decisions</h3><div class="quiz-list">
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The clue sounds familiar, but the speaker cannot fully identify it." data-hint="Choose partial recognition rather than complete memory or literal sound." data-fix="Rings a bell means that something seems familiar."><p class="quiz-question">1. In “The project name rings a bell, but I cannot place it,” what does the idiom contribute?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The clue sounds familiar, but the speaker cannot fully identify it.">The clue sounds familiar, but the speaker cannot fully identify it.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A physical bell rings whenever the project begins.">A physical bell rings whenever the project begins.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The speaker remembers every detail with certainty.">The speaker remembers every detail with certainty.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="I draw the line at excluding the accessibility reviewer." data-hint="Use the boundary expression with at plus an -ing complement." data-fix="Draw the line at introduces the unacceptable action."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which sentence uses the fixed boundary grammar?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="I draw the line at excluding the accessibility reviewer.">I draw the line at excluding the accessibility reviewer.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="I draw the line to exclude at the reviewer.">I draw the line to exclude at the reviewer.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="I draw a lines on excluding reviewer.">I draw a lines on excluding reviewer.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Grain of salt is the common U.S. target, while pinch of salt is also an established variant in other English use." data-hint="Describe the target variety and established variation without inventing an error." data-fix="Teach grain for the U.S. target and recognize established pinch variation."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which statement handles variety responsibly?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Grain of salt is the common U.S. target, while pinch of salt is also an established variant in other English use.">Grain of salt is the common U.S. target, while pinch of salt is also an established variant in other English use.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pinch of salt is grammatically impossible in every English variety.">Pinch of salt is grammatically impossible in every English variety.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every English variety must use a different meaning for salt.">Every English variety must use a different meaning for salt.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The measured clearance was one inch below the stated requirement; the source does not identify why." data-hint="Use literal evidence because the source does not establish deliberate sacrifice or motive." data-fix="The formal record should state the measurement and evidence boundary."><p class="quiz-question">4. Which sentence belongs in the formal inspection record?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The measured clearance was one inch below the stated requirement; the source does not identify why.">The measured clearance was one inch below the stated requirement; the source does not identify why.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Those terrible corner-cutters obviously planned every failure.">Those terrible corner-cutters obviously planned every failure.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The ramp was back to square one and rang every bell.">The ramp was back to square one and rang every bell.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Calling a supported learner's task “not rocket science” may minimize the difficulty and effort; a literal description is safer." data-hint="Evaluate the interpersonal effect, not only the dictionary meaning." data-fix="The cliché can sound dismissive when directed at someone who struggled."><p class="quiz-question">5. Why might “It's not rocket science” be a poor response here?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Calling a supported learner's task “not rocket science” may minimize the difficulty and effort; a literal description is safer.">Calling a supported learner's task “not rocket science” may minimize the difficulty and effort; a literal description is safer.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The phrase is always a formal scientific measurement.">The phrase is always a formal scientific measurement.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The phrase can only describe an actual spacecraft.">The phrase can only describe an actual spacecraft.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Zero duplicates in staging supports “so far, so good,” but it does not guarantee live reliability." data-hint="Keep the successful checkpoint and future-use boundary together." data-fix="The expression reports current progress, not a permanent guarantee."><p class="quiz-question">6. Which Alder Street conclusion is calibrated?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Zero duplicates in staging supports “so far, so good,” but it does not guarantee live reliability.">Zero duplicates in staging supports “so far, so good,” but it does not guarantee live reliability.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="So far, so good proves that every live order will succeed forever.">So far, so good proves that every live order will succeed forever.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The phrase means the staging test definitely failed.">The phrase means the staging test definitely failed.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The pilot provides limited evidence supporting a larger trial; it does not establish full effectiveness." data-hint="Match the B2 writing assessment's evidence, degree, register, and recommendation requirements." data-fix="Use literal evidence-calibrated wording in the formal recommendation."><p class="quiz-question">7. Which sentence best fits the B2 evidence-based writing assessment?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The pilot provides limited evidence supporting a larger trial; it does not establish full effectiveness.">The pilot provides limited evidence supporting a larger trial; it does not establish full effectiveness.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="At the end of the day, the awesome pilot hit it out of the park.">At the end of the day, the awesome pilot hit it out of the park.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The piece-of-cake trial changed the game forever, no questions asked.">The piece-of-cake trial changed the game forever, no questions asked.</button></div></div>
  </div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p></div>

  <h2>Reflect: did the expression improve the message?</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Explain the choice, not just the definition</h3><p>Complete these lines aloud: <span class="ex">The literal reading would be...</span> <span class="ex">The context instead shows...</span> <span class="ex">The speaker's purpose is...</span> <span class="ex">The fixed grammar is...</span> <span class="ex">The flexible slot is...</span> <span class="ex">The phrase implies...</span> <span class="ex">The register fits because...</span> <span class="ex">The plain alternative is...</span> <span class="ex">The phrase would overstate...</span> <span class="ex">The listener still needs to know...</span></p><p>Then change the audience, channel, stakes, evidence, or relationship. Keep, modify, paraphrase, or remove the idiom according to its new communicative value.</p><p><a href="#next-day-retrieval">Return tomorrow for the five-item retrieval check</a> before rereading the lesson.</p></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Next use:</b> Notice one idiom in a real but nonprivate conversation, program, or article. Record its complete sentence, plain paraphrase, grammar pattern, speaker relationship, and whether you would actively use it.</div>
